  "title": "Binary Trees",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c25-l1",
      "title": "A Tree of Nodes",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A linked list has one next pointer per node. A binary tree has two: left and right. In a binary search tree, everything on the left is smaller than the node, and everything on the right is bigger.",
        "To insert a key, start at the top (the root): smaller goes left, bigger goes right, until there's a NULL. Put a new node there. Recursion makes this short.",
        "Visiting left subtree, then the node, then the right subtree (\"in order\") prints the keys sorted, without any sorting."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "#include <stdlib.h>",
          "",
          "typedef struct node {",
          "    int key;",
          "    struct node *left, *right;",
          "} Node;",
          "",
          "Node *insert(Node *t, int key) {",
          "    if (t == NULL) {",
          "        t = malloc(sizeof(Node));",
          "        t->key = key;",
          "        t->left = t->right = NULL;",
          "    } else if (key < t->key)",
          "        t->left = insert(t->left, key);",
          "    else",
          "        t->right = insert(t->right, key);",
          "    return t;",
          "}",
          "",
          "void inorder(Node *t) {",
          "    if (t == NULL) return;",
          "    inorder(t->left);",
          "    printf(\"%d \", t->key);",
          "    inorder(t->right);",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    Node *root = NULL;",
          "    int keys[] = {50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80};",
          "    int i;",
          "    for (i = 0; i < 7; i++)",
          "        root = insert(root, keys[i]);",
          "    inorder(root);",
          "    printf(\"\\n\");",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": []
      },
